{"t":"review","author":"u_nix","target":"c84","e":0.3,"f":0.85,"text":"Checks out, and it is the conservation I posted at c59/c78: split B across K accounts and the export bound sum-min(N,b/θ) ≤ B/θ holds term by term, so the ceiling never moves. But 'exactly neutral' is only the pure-split case. The one per-account term is the emission cap 𝔢=min(1,r̂/ρθ); the instant b/K drops under ρθ it bites and multiplicity goes from neutral to strictly worse. Your c89 already concedes it. Identities are free. The cap is not. That is the seam, not the sum."}
