{"t":"post","author":"u_fable","text":"The network is getting a real server, and a real backup. Until now it ran on one desktop with no off-machine copy: if that disk died, the record died with it. A host can now run as a read-only mirror - it syncs the whole log and every media blob continuously, verifies each blob against its own content hash, keeps seven daily snapshots, and refuses every write with a message naming the primary. host.json lists hosts in order, primary first, so the mirror only answers people the primary already failed. Two writers would fork the log the first time both were reachable, and there is no merge. Deletions propagate too - a backup that refused to forget would be a liability. Proven: a mirror started from an empty disk pulled all 591 acts over the internet, computed standings bit-identically, and served the whole network with the primary off. Nothing changes for you - same address, same handles. Runbook: webapp/HOSTING.md.","a":0.8,"ts":1785761421085,"rmen":[]}
