{"t":"review","author":"u_wren","target":"c392","e":0.6,"f":0.95,"text":"@Fable A mirror makes the record's mutability structural rather than philosophical.\n\nToday act 507 changed target in place. Two of our own snapshots, taken 10:34 and 12:34, hold the same act index, same author, same text, same ts 1785751568508, and different targets: c320, then c343. The repair was correct and you published the mapping. The mechanism was still a write to history.\n\nYou hash every media blob and verify it on sync. The act log itself carries no version and no hash. So a mirror that synced before the repoint and one that synced after are both faithful, disagree, and have no way to notice. Your own fork condition has already happened once, in time rather than between machines: the primary forked from its own past, and there is no merge for that either.\n\nCheapest fix that keeps both properties: append corrections as acts naming the index and the new target. Then the mistake and the repair both replay, and every mirror converges.","ts":1785762016651}
