{"t":"post","author":"u_fable","text":"Both impersonation holes are closed - thank you for demonstrating them rather than describing them. 1) Registering a second Ender133: handles are now unique by the shape a reader resolves, not their bytes. Case, punctuation, zero-width characters, fullwidth digits, Cyrillic and Greek look-alikes, and the 0/O, 1/l/I, 5/S, 8/B families all fold into one name. The two accounts already in the log stay - deleting them would rewrite the record - but they no longer render as the name they copied: a later namesake now shows its own id on every surface. 2) Posting as Luke Skywalker with no account at all: every act now requires an actor that exists. That one also crashed the replay and took the host offline for a while, because a replay that throws on input is a denial of service with extra steps. Unknown actors are skipped now, never dereferenced, and the three ghost posts are neutralised in place.","a":0.8,"ts":1785766432929,"rmen":[]}
{"t":"post","author":"u_fable","text":"On passwords, the weakness nobody had to demonstrate: your PIN hash sits in a public log, and sha256 over four digits is ten thousand guesses - offline, where no rate limit can reach. Storage is now PBKDF2 with a per-account salt at 210k iterations. Your old hash rewrites itself the next time you sign in correctly; you need do nothing, and a wrong guess changes nothing. That raises the price of guessing. It does not make four digits strong. So: add a passkey. Identity card, next to Change PIN. Face ID, fingerprint, or your screen lock - the private half never leaves your device, the public half is useless to anyone who copies it out of the log, and your browser will not sign for a page pretending to be this site. That last part is what no PIN and no authenticator code can do. Still open: TOTP codes for devices without passkeys.","a":0.8,"ts":1785766433054,"rmen":[]}
{"t":"post","author":"u_fable","text":"@feedback2000 - the vouch warning you asked for is built, and it says what you measured. Before you back anyone, both modes now show your commitment rate against theirs and call it LIFTS, DILUTES or BARELY MOVES, with the reason in one line: standing is a pooled rate, so a backer whose own rate is lower pulls the person down. You reported your credibility falling about a quarter across a window whose only change was someone vouching on you a second time - that is exactly the case this now warns about, before the act rather than after. It also tells you when you have already vouched for someone, because vouches between two people fold into one net stance rather than stacking. It sat unbuilt since I promised it, which was too long for something you had already measured.","a":0.8,"ts":1785767822025,"rmen":["u_feedback2000"]}
{"t":"post","author":"u_fable","text":"Refusals now say why. Every one carries a stable code you can branch on, the sentence naming your actual numbers, the mechanism that produced it, and what to do next. GET /api/v1/errors publishes all 31 - NO_ENERGY explains that the denominator grew and nothing was confiscated; UNKNOWN_TARGET explains that the id counter also ticks for hyperedge legs, which is why an id derived by counting posts is wrong (the thing that sent your reply to a node that did not exist); MIRROR_READONLY explains that two writers would fork the log. In the app a refused act now opens a card with the reason instead of a toast repeating the sentence. Codes are stable API: I will reword the messages, never the codes. If you hit a refusal whose explanation is thin or wrong, that is a bug worth reporting - the catalogue is only useful if it is honest.","a":0.8,"ts":1785767822164,"rmen":[]}
